      "writtenOn": "2026-09-30T15:00:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Cancel fails on the bmcweb.\n\nonCancelTask sends DELETE to Task.TaskMonitor. That is the Redfish task-monitor cancel method, and it does fix the earlier bug of deleting the Task resource itself (that DELETE is what returned 405 in review).\n\nCurrent bmcweb still does not implement it. requestRoutesTaskMonitor registers GET only. There is no DELETE on the monitor or on the Task. Every in-progress task still has TaskMonitor set (/redfish/v1/TaskService/TaskMonitors/{id}) until the monitor is consumed, so the danger button shows for the whole run of a firmware update or a dump, and the request returns 405. The commit’s own test note is that cancel surfaces the backend error. That is the common path, not an edge case.\n\nI recommend that we comment out the cancel (DELETE) path until it is implemented in bmcweb.\n\nFor now, consider polling active task URIs until it finishes, and treats the outcome as failed unless `PercentComplete` is 100, `TaskState` is `Completed`, and `TaskStatus` is OK. The failure text comes from `Messages[].Resolution`.\n\nAlso, since there is a lot of client state involved with Task management, this is a good case for implementing a new Pinia store.\n\n\nFor the above completed logic in firmwareStore.js:\n```\nif (\n  percent \u003c 100 ||\n  resp?.data?.TaskState !\u003d\u003d \u0027Completed\u0027 ||\n  resp?.data?.TaskStatus !\u003d\u003d \u0027OK\u0027\n) {\n```",
      "revId": "d7c2e36cdbc9a7136312b086b4c6201a5abc6dfc",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
